public class Singleton {
    private static volatile Singleton instance; // volatile is REQUIRED
    private Singleton() {}
    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {              // first check — no lock
            synchronized (Singleton.class) {
                if (instance == null) {      // second check — with lock
                    instance = new Singleton();
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}

Why volatile: object creation (new Singleton()) has 3 steps: (1) allocate memory, (2) initialise fields, (3) assign reference to 'instance'. The JVM/CPU can reorder steps 2 and 3. Without volatile, another thread could see instance != null (step 3 done) but with uninitialised fields (step 2 not done yet). Volatile prevents reordering — guarantees the reference is only published after full initialisation.

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class LatchDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        int workerCount = 5;
        CountDownLatch latch = new CountDownLatch(workerCount);
        ExecutorService exec = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(workerCount);

        for (int i = 1; i <= workerCount; i++) {
            final int id = i;
            exec.submit(() -> {
                try {
                    System.out.println("Worker-" + id + " initialising...");
                    Thread.sleep((long)(Math.random() * 1000)); // simulate init
                    System.out.println("Worker-" + id + " ready");
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                } finally {
                    latch.countDown(); // signal completion — even on exception
                }
            });
        }
        System.out.println("Main: waiting for all workers to initialise...");
        latch.await(); // blocks until count reaches 0
        System.out.println("Main: all workers ready — proceeding");
        exec.shutdown();
    }
}

